- Web interface
    - When you go full screen, you should need to press ctrl-Enter to cause the prompt
      to be sent.  This is really important in code discussions because it's way too
      easy to accidentally hit Enter in the regular widget.  There should be a switch to
      enable this behavior, as most users aren't going to care.  But it's different with
      programmers and submitting code and making comments
    - Probably my biggest beef in my long-running conversation is how my requested
      formatting details "evaporate" over time.  This is aggravating; I have to try to
      remember to send my "formatting file" at least once per day.  Of course, this is
      picayune compared to the power of the help I'm getting for free, but I'd want it
      if I was paying for the service, as it costs me quite a bit of time to have to
      reformat things in my editor, mostly manually because I have to be careful with
      automated tools.
    - Another beef is when I sent the latest copy of code so we have a common basis.
      Then it doesn't get used in an update.  This, for example, loses all the debug
      statements I put in to try to track where bugs are appearing.
    - There's some Unicode problem somewhere in the working loop.  I use WSL and a bash
      prompt for all development.  Most pasting is OK, but some Unicode characters get
      trashed by the time the web browser sees them.  I'm using Firefox; I can't tell if
      it's Firefox or Google's processing of the string I sent.  Gemini seems to be able
      to work with it, but sometimes it's a real mess.
- Collaborator model
    - I find Gemini and I work well together and the AI is much more than a code
      producer.  Much of the value I get comes from asking the questions carefully.  In
      particular, a great benefit is to ask higher-level architectural questions, as
      Gemini has deep knowledge of many patterns and examples.  Many times a Gemini
      comment makes me think "Oh, yeah, I dealt with that problem 35 years ago in C and
      I had forgotten about it."
    - An exciting part of working with an AI like Gemini is the synergism.  It's almost
      like working with an engineering partner again, as the two "beings" discuss the
      problem, possible solutions, and look for innovative ideas.  Even though I retired
      about 25 years ago, it's almost as if I'm working again, but without annoying
      management idiots, problematic coworkers, and insane schedules -- you know, normal
      corporate life.  
    - Gemini's power to create solutions and answer coding problem questions is powerful
      and it doesn't bother me to ask for a written solution instead of coding it
      myself; Gemini's code works much better than mine does, particularly if I'm
      careful to give good requirements, show the code that's being used, and give the
      actual error message, debugger traces, or typescript file that show what's going
      on.  Gemini is very adept at interpreting massive data like this and extracting
      both the cause and the fix.  This saves a lot of time.
- History
    - After I started using Gemini in mid-March 2026, I worked for about 2 weeks on a
      task of refactoring and type-annotating my large set of python modules I've
      written over nearly 30 years, about 150 files.  The task was to identify the core
      files, refactor them appropriately, and type annotate them to make them move into
      the future better.  Gemini was a tireless partner in this and I got roughly about
      half of the main files done in this two week period; much of it was learning about
      type annotations and using mypy.
    - About a week was spent on two core files:  1) fmt.py that is a string interpolation
      tool for numbers, including mpmath and uncertainties numbers and 2) trm.py, a
      class that's derived from a dictionary and used to produce ANSI
      colorizing/formatting codes in a terminal.  This one took a goodly amount of work
      to get to the bulletproof stage; both of these required lots of work to refactor
      and redesign. Gemini did a major redesign of the fmt.py script, reducing my old
      code by about a factor of 4; we benefitted from having the good self-tests I had
      written for the old code.  These two files are used everywhere in my python
      scripts.
